The complete corresponding Mealie source, including its frontend, build files,
Python dependency lock and frontend package lock, is downloaded and SHA-256
verified by environment/fetch.py at build time as mealie-3.29.0-source.tar.gz
from https://github.com/mealie-recipes/mealie/archive/refs/tags/v3.29.0.tar.gz.
It is not bundled in the Git checkout. The reference image retains this archive
and the libxcrypt source archive described below under /solution/.

The runtime archive contains the unmodified application, compiled frontend,
Python interpreter, Python packages, NLTK data and required shared libraries
from the official Linux amd64 image identified in SOURCE.json. The package
layout is relocated; start.sh supplies documented Mealie environment settings.
No application behavior or source is patched. Python package metadata and their
bundled licenses remain in the runtime's site-packages directories. Python
3.14.8 is under the Python Software Foundation License; OpenSSL 3.5.7 under
Apache-2.0; SQLite 3.46.1 is public domain; OpenLDAP 2.6.10 under the OpenLDAP
Public License; Cyrus SASL 2.1.28 under its BSD-style license. These are the
interpreter and the shared libraries in mealie/lib. See the official image and
corresponding upstream projects for these components' source and distribution
notices.

Native libraries in the Python wheels

Binary wheels in the runtime include native libraries; they are unchanged.
numpy 2.5.3 (numpy.libs: OpenBLAS with LAPACK, libgfortran, libquadmath) and
Pillow 12.3.0 (pillow.libs) list their bundled libraries with licenses and
source locations in LICENSES/numpy-2.5.3.txt and LICENSES/pillow-12.3.0.txt.

The pillow_heif 1.8.1 source code is BSD-3-Clause; its binary wheel as a whole
is GPL-2.0 because of the libraries it bundles in pillow_heif.libs (see the
wheel's LICENSES_bundled.txt in LICENSES/pillow_heif-1.8.1.txt). Corresponding
source:
  libheif 1.23.6   LGPL-3.0  https://github.com/strukturag/libheif/tree/v1.23.6
  libde265 1.1.3   LGPL-3.0  https://github.com/strukturag/libde265/tree/v1.1.3
  x265 4.3         GPL-2.0   https://github.com/Multicorewareinc/x265/tree/4.3
x265 reports its build as 4.3+1-e9b8812. The pillow_heif 1.8.1 source is at
https://github.com/bigcat88/pillow_heif and
https://pypi.org/project/pillow-heif/1.8.1/ .

psycopg2-binary 2.9.13 is LGPL-3.0-or-later with an OpenSSL linking exception
(LICENSES/psycopg2_binary-2.9.13.txt); its source is at
https://github.com/psycopg/psycopg2 and
https://pypi.org/project/psycopg2-binary/2.9.13/ . It bundles these libraries
in psycopg2_binary.libs:
  libpq                        PostgreSQL 17.11      PostgreSQL License
  libssl, libcrypto            OpenSSL 3.5.8         Apache-2.0
  libldap, liblber             OpenLDAP 2.6.14       OpenLDAP Public License 2.8
  libsasl2                     Cyrus SASL 2.1.26     BSD-style (CMU)
  libgssapi_krb5, libkrb5,     MIT Kerberos 1.15.1   MIT and other permissive
    libk5crypto, libkrb5support                      licenses
  libcom_err                   e2fsprogs 1.42.9      permissive (MIT SIPB)
  libkeyutils                  keyutils 1.5.8        LGPL-2.0-or-later
  libselinux                   libselinux 2.5        public domain
  libpcre                      PCRE 8.32             BSD-3-Clause
  libcrypt                     libxcrypt 4.5.2       LGPL-2.1-or-later
Cyrus SASL, MIT Kerberos, e2fsprogs, keyutils, libselinux and PCRE are the
CentOS 7 packages recorded in the wheel's SBOM: cyrus-sasl-lib-2.1.26-24.el7_9,
krb5-libs-1.15.1-55.el7_9, libcom_err-1.42.9-19.el7, keyutils-libs-1.5.8-3.el7,
libselinux-2.5-15.el7 and pcre-8.32-17.el7. Their source RPMs are in the CentOS
vault, https://vault.centos.org/7.9.2009/ ; keyutils 1.5.8 is also available
from http://people.redhat.com/~dhowells/keyutils/ .

The libcrypt-bc2db45e.so.1 library is libxcrypt 4.5.2. Its bytes match the
library in the official psycopg2-binary 2.9.13 CPython 3.14 manylinux2014
x86_64 wheel. That release builds with cibuildwheel 4.2.0, whose pinned image
is quay.io/pypa/manylinux2014_x86_64@sha256:c163a278006f45883c117b4166b93cdc7b574b62e58292ed9e52879579841d77.
The image's libxcrypt build layer identifies version 4.5.2 and the source hash;
its original libcrypt.so.1 has SHA-256
bc2db45ec146e845246bd2610dd4d8bf8c9176adda585ba15f51dd30dbf36459.
The original and wheel libraries have identical code, read-only data and
relocation sections; auditwheel changes the library name and ELF metadata.
SOURCE.json and LICENSES/libxcrypt-4.5.2-provenance.json record these hashes
and the fixed upstream build references.

Corresponding libxcrypt source:
https://github.com/besser82/libxcrypt/releases/download/v4.5.2/libxcrypt-4.5.2.tar.xz
  669820 bytes, SHA-256
  71513a31c01a428bccd5367a32fd95f115d6dac50fb5b60c779d5c7942aec071
The build downloads and verifies this archive and retains it in the reference
image at /solution/. The manylinux build recipe, including its glibc-compatible
symbol-map adjustment and configure flags, is reproduced in
LICENSES/libxcrypt-4.5.2-build.txt. Exact release notices and the complete LGPL
text are in LICENSES/libxcrypt-4.5.2.txt.

Some extension modules link libraries statically. lxml 6.1.3 includes libxml2
2.14.6, libxslt 1.1.43 with libexslt, zlib 1.3.2 and GNU libiconv 1.18
(LICENSES/lxml-6.1.3.txt); libiconv is LGPL-2.1 and its source is
https://ftp.gnu.org/gnu/libiconv/libiconv-1.18.tar.gz . curl_cffi 0.16.3
includes libcurl 8.21.0 from curl-impersonate with BoringSSL, zlib 1.3.1,
brotli 1.2.0, zstd 1.5.7, nghttp2 1.63.0, ngtcp2 1.20.0, nghttp3 1.15.0 and
libidn2 2.3.7; libidn2 is GPL-2.0-or-later or LGPL-3.0-or-later and its source
is https://ftp.gnu.org/gnu/libidn/libidn2-2.3.7.tar.gz , while the other
libraries are under permissive licenses. Also linked statically are OpenSSL
4.0.2 in cryptography 50.0.1 (Apache-2.0), and libyaml 0.2.5 in PyYAML 6.0.3,
libuv 1.48.0 in uvloop 0.22.1 and libffi in cffi 2.1.1 (all MIT). The Rust
extensions (cryptography, jiter, orjson, pydantic_core, watchfiles) list their
crates and licenses in the SBOMs in their dist-info/sboms directories.

LICENSES/native-libraries.txt contains the PostgreSQL, OpenLDAP, Cyrus SASL,
MIT Kerberos, com_err, keyutils, libselinux and PCRE licenses, as well as the
Apache License 2.0, GNU GPL versions 2 and 3, and GNU LGPL versions 2.1 and 3.
It also points to the exact libxcrypt 4.5.2 notices in libxcrypt-4.5.2.txt.
Where the license file of an exact bundled release was not available,
it gives the project's license as distributed by Debian or Ubuntu.

